image-plan:
  - file: Relief_Ur-Nanshe_Louvre_AO2344.jpg
    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: "Ur-Nanshe at work on a temple: at the top he creates the foundation
      for a shrine, at the bottom he presides over the dedication. Almost all
      his known inscriptions deal with the construction of temples. Louvre."
  - file: Sumer_map.jpg
    role: full
    section: history
    caption: Lagash in green, before the expansion of the Akkadian Empire; the
      territory of Sumer appears in orange. Circa 2350 BC.
  - file: P1130735_Louvre_stèle_des_Vautours_rwk.JPG
    role: full
    section: eannatum
    caption: Eannatum, King of Lagash, riding a war chariot, in a detail of the
      Stele of the Vultures. His name "Eannatum" (𒂍𒀭𒈾𒁺) is written
      vertically in two columns in front of his head. Louvre Museum.
  - file: This_cuneiform_text_gives_the_city_of_Umma's_account_of_its_long-running_border_dispute_with_Lagash._Circa_2350_BCE._From_Umma,_Iraq._The_British_Museum,_London.jpg
    role: inline
    section: eannatum
    caption: "Umma's side of the argument: a vase of King Gishakidu, king of Umma
      and son of Ur-Lumma, giving the city of Umma's account of the
      long-running border dispute with Lagash. It redefines the frontier by
      recording the locations of stelae to the god Shara, and the distances
      between them. Circa 2350 BC, from Umma, Iraq. Ref. 140889, British
      Museum, London."
  - file: Vase_Entemena_Louvre_AO2674.jpg
    role: inline
    section: entemena
    caption: Entemena's inscribed silver vase, c. 2400 BC. Louvre.
  - file: Vessel_fragment_depicting_the_goddess_Nisaba_(?)_and_an_inscription_from_Entemena,_ruler_of_Lagash,_circa_2430_BC,_chlorite,_Pergamon_Museum,_Berlin.jpg
    role: inline
    section: entemena
    caption: The goddess Nisaba, on a vessel fragment carrying an inscription of
      Entemena, ruler of Lagash (2430 BC). Steatite; Vorderasiatisches Museum
      Berlin.
  - file: Gudea_of_Lagash_Girsu.jpg
    role: full
    section: gudea
    caption: Gudea of Lagash, who ruled c. 2144–2124 BC. Diorite statue found at
      Girsu. Louvre Museum.
  - file: Hammurabi's_Babylonia_1.svg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: At the time of Hammurabi, Lagash was located near the shoreline of
      the gulf.
  - file: Tell_al_Hiba_(22795101268).jpg
    role: full
    section: archaeology
    caption: "What is left: one of the largest archaeological sites in the region,
      and at its highest only 6 meters above the plain."
  - file: Gudea_Lagashki_inscription_in_linear_script_and_cuneiform_script_on_clay.jpg
    role: inline
    section: archaeology
    caption: The name Lagash Ki (𒉢𒁓𒆷𒆠, "Country of Lagash") on inscriptions of
      Gudea, in monumental linear script and cuneiform script on clay.
  - file: 3-D_reconstruction_of_Area_A.png
    role: inline
    section: level-i-architecture
    caption: "Area A reconstructed by Keifuhui, from the front: the oval wall
      enclosing the courtyard, with the temple-building reached by steps."
  - file: 3-D_reconstruction_of_Area_A_by_Keifuhui.png
    role: inline
    section: level-i-architecture
    caption: Another view of the same reconstruction of Area A by Keifuhui.
  - file: 3-D_reconstruction_of_Area_B_by_Dcldeobi.png
    role: inline
    section: the-3hb-building
    caption: Area B reconstructed by Dcldeobi, from the front.
  - file: 3-D_reconstruction_of_Area_B_by_Dcldeobi_(Back).png
    role: inline
    section: the-4hb-building
    caption: The same reconstruction of Area B by Dcldeobi, seen from the back.
  - file: 3-D_reconstruction_of_Area_G.jpg
    role: inline
    section: area-g
    caption: "Area G reconstructed by Nic9137: a building complex and a curving
      wall, separated by around 30-40m."
  - file: 3-D_reconstruction_of_Area_G_of_Lagash.jpg
    role: inline
    section: western-building-complex
    caption: Another view of Area G by Nic9137.
further:
  - slug: akkadian-empire
    why: Lagash was under its control for several centuries, and Rimush's
      reconquest cost the cities of Ur and Lagash 8,049 killed by his own
      record.
  - slug: gutian-rule-in-mesopotamia
    why: Whether Gudea's rich, independent Lagash sat inside the Gutian century or
      after it is the article's live argument.
  - slug: mesopotamia
    why: Eannatum turned Lagash into a major power extending throughout large
      areas of it.
